## What the MIT grant above covers, and what it does not

The MIT licence above applies to the **software** in this repository: the
application source, the Terraform under `iac/`, the build and prerender
scripts, the CI workflows, the tests, and the configuration. Fork it, adapt it,
ship it commercially — that is the intent, and `/architecture` says so out
loud: *"fork the two repos, read the ADRs, adopt the skills plugin as your
loop, then swap in your own content and positioning."*

It does **not** cover the **editorial content**: the author's writing, the
images of him, and his professional record — published here to be read, not
relicensed. **The rule is what governs, not the list.** Anything that is the
author's own words or likeness is reserved wherever in the tree it lives; the
paths below are an illustrative aid and are deliberately not exhaustive, because
a list always lags the next file nobody thought to add.

- `apps/fed/src/content/**` — the articles, the ramp-up plan, the architecture
  page
- `apps/fed/src/data/profile.ts` — the CV
- `apps/fed/src/data/catalog.ts`, `apps/fed/src/data/repoCards.ts` — the
  portfolio copy
- `apps/fed/src/i18n/messages.ts` — the site's UI **strings** (the surrounding
  module — its keys, types and the `satisfies` check that makes a missing
  translation a compile error — is software, and is MIT like the rest)
- `apps/fed/src/lib/site.ts`, `apps/fed/src/components/contactChannels.ts`,
  `apps/fed/index.html`'s meta tags — the meta/OG copy and the public contact
  details
- `apps/fed/public/og-default.png` and any portrait of the author

Those are **© 2026 Luiz Tadeu Mendonça**, all rights reserved. Quote them with
attribution as you would any published writing; do not republish them wholesale
or present them as your own.

**Two things under `apps/fed/public/` are neither granted nor reserved here,
because they are not the author's to grant or reserve.** The certification
badges in `apps/fed/public/badges/` are the **issuers' trademarks and
artwork** — AWS's, reproduced under the issuers' own terms. They identify
credentials held by the author; they are not part of this repository's licence
in either direction, and a forker should delete them rather than reuse them.
Everything else in that directory — `robots.txt`, `favicon.svg`, the touch
icon — is functional and is MIT with the rest of the software.

The split follows the principle stated at the end of `/architecture`'s
*"Replicate it for your own context"* section: *"fork the two repos, read the
ADRs … then swap in your own content and positioning. The structure carries;
the words are yours."* That line is an invitation, not a licence — this file is
the licence — but it names the same boundary. The structure is what is being
given away. The words are not, because a CV and a body of writing under a
permissive software licence could be reposted verbatim by anyone, including
under someone else's name.
